<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ferdinand von Zeppelin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1838">1838</a>–<a href="/years/1917">1917</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Count Zeppelin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Count Ferdinand von Zeppelin was a German army officer from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstanz">Konstanz</a> who developed the rigid airship. His first airship, LZ 1, flew over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a> from Friedrichshafen in 1900, and his company Luftschiffbau Zeppelin built airships used for passenger travel and as bombers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. His name became the common word for an airship.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdinand-von-zeppelin.jpg" alt="Ferdinand von Zeppelin" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicola Perscheid · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bildnis_Ferdinand_von_Zeppelin.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
